{"id":"f118c22a-1d0d-4b23-a2c5-9429fcb83aa2","object":"chat.completion","created":1784818061,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import re\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'lines' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing 'lines'\\\")\\n    lines = case['lines']\\n    if not isinstance(lines, list) or not lines:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: lines must be non-empty list\\\")\\n    trajectory = []\\n    valid = True\\n    violation_line = None\\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\\n        match = re.match(r'^(Thought|Action|Action Input|Observation|Final):\\\\s*(.*)$', line, re.IGNORECASE)\\n        if not match:\\n            valid = False\\n            violation_line = i\\n            break\\n        type_str = match.group(1)\\n        content = match.group(2).strip()\\n        type_lower = type_str.lower().replace(' ', '_')\\n        if type_lower not in ('thought', 'action', 'action_input', 'observation', 'final'):\\n            valid = False\\n            violation_line = i\\n            break\\n        if i == 1 and type_lower != 'thought':\\n            valid = False\\n            violation_line = i\\n            break\\n        if prev_type is None:\\n            allowed = ['thought']\\n        else:\\n            if prev_type == 'thought':\\n                allowed = ['action', 'final']\\n            elif prev_type == 'action':\\n                allowed = ['observation', 'action_input']\\n            elif prev_type == 'action_input':\\n                allowed = ['observation']\\n            elif prev_type == 'observation':\\n                allowed = ['thought']\\n            elif prev_type == 'final':\\n                allowed = []\\n            else:\\n                allowed = []\\n        if type_lower not in allowed:\\n            valid = False\\n            violation_line = i\\n            break\\n        record = {'type': type_lower, 'content': content, 'line': i}\\n        trajectory.append(record)\\n        prev_type = type_lower\\n    if valid and trajectory:\\n        last_type = trajectory[-1]['type']\\n        if last_type != 'final':\\n            valid = False\\n            violation_line = len(lines)\\n            trajectory = trajectory[:-1]\\n    if valid:\\n        return {'valid': True, 'trajectory': trajectory}\\n    else:\\n        return {'valid': False, 'violation_line': violation_line, 'valid_prefix': trajectory, 'trajectory': trajectory}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that parses and validates ReAct trajectories. The input case has a list of lines. We need to parse each line, check for valid sequence: Thought, Action, Observation (nonterminal cycles) and Final only at end. Also need to validate tool schema? The problem mentions \"registered tool schema\" but no specific tools are defined in the fixture. Possibly it's about action types? The problem statement: \"Parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records under a registered tool schema and stop grammar.\" However, the fixture only shows lines like \"Thought: inspect\", \"Action: lookup\", \"Observation: found\", \"Final: done\". There is no Action Input in the example. But the description mentions Action Input. Possibly we need to handle lines that may include Action Input? But the fixture doesn't show it. The task says \"use only quantities present in the case payload\". So we only have these lines. The lines are of format \"Type: content\". We need to parse each line. The types are: Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation, Final. But the example lines don't have Action Input. Possibly we need to recognize them if present. Also, \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So sequence must be: Thought, Action, Observation, then repeat Thought, etc. Final can only be at the end. Also \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" That means no extra lines after Final.\n\nWe need to return \"lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" So likely we need to output a list of records with type, content, line number? And a valid flag. Also \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" - maybe include case_id? But the function signature is solve(case) where case is dict. Return JSON-serializable object. Probably we need to return something like:\n{\n  \"valid\": true/false,\n  \"trajectory\": [{\"type\": \"thought\", \"content\": \"inspect\", \"line\": 1}, ...]\n}\nAnd if invalid, return valid false and maybe error line and valid prefix. Also \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we need to return the line number where violation occurs and the valid prefix (the trajectory up to that point?).\n\nAlso note: \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" The case payload is given, but we are to parse and validate. If case is malformed (e.g., not a dict, missing lines), raise ValueError. But the fixture is valid.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Parse each line: split by \": \" to get type and content. Type should be one of: Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation, Final. Content is the rest.\n- Keep track of expected next type. The sequence: after Thought, expect Action (or possibly Action Input? Actually description says \"Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation\". But typical ReAct: Thought, Action, Observation. Sometimes Action Input is part of Action? The line format: \"Action: lookup\" then \"Action Input: search query\"? Not in fixture. Possibly we need to allow Action Input optionally after Action? The problem says \"Interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation\". So sequence might be: Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation. But also there is \"Final\". But the fixture lines don't have Action Input. Possibly the tool schema defines that some actions require input, but not in this case. We need to be flexible. The task says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" And \"registered tool schema\" but no schema given. Possibly we only need to enforce ordering: a cycle is Thought -> Action -> Observation. But Action Input may appear optionally after Action? However, the line \"Action: lookup\" does not have Action Input, so maybe it's valid. Alternatively, maybe Action Input is a separate line that must follow Action if the action requires input? But we don't have that info. Given the fixture, we have only Thought, Action, Observation, Final. So we should implement a state machine that allows:\n- Start: expecting Thought or Final? Actually \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So first must be Thought. Final can only appear at the end, and after a thought? Possibly Final can be after an Observation? The description: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" So after an Observation, next could be Final? Or after a Thought? In typical ReAct, Final is used to output final answer after reasoning. The example shows \"Thought: answer\" then \"Final: done\". That is: Thought then Final. Is that allowed? The requirement says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" That implies nonterminal cycles are Thought->Action->Observation. Then after Observation, you can have another Thought to start next cycle, or Final to stop. So Final can come after Observation or after Thought? In the example, after \"Thought: answer\", they have \"Final: done\". There is no Action and Observation after that thought. That is a terminal cycle? Actually the sequence is: Thought(inspect), Action(lookup), Observation(found), Thought(answer), Final(done). So the last cycle is just Thought then Final. That seems allowed because Final is at the end. So the state machine should allow: after a Thought, either Action (nonterminal) or Final (terminal). After Action, must be Observation. After Observation, must be Thought (next cycle) or Final? But in example, after Observation(found) they have Thought(answer) not Final. So after Observation, you can have Thought. Then from Thought you can go to Action or Final. So Final can only appear after a Thought, not after Action or Observation directly. That matches \"only at a reasoning boundary\" - reasoning boundary could be after a thought? Or after observation? Usually reasoning ends after a thought that concludes. So I'll implement: after Thought, allowed next: Action or Final. After Action, allowed next: Observation (or possibly Action Input? We'll handle Action Input as optional between Action and Observation). After Observation, allowed next: Thought (or Final? I'll disallow Final after Observation directly, but check the spec: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" I think Final can only appear after a Thought, because the thought is the reasoning that leads to the final answer. So I'll enforce that. Also, if Action Input appears, it must follow Action, and then must be followed by Observation.\n\nWe also need to check that after Final, no more lines.\n\nDefine a state machine with states: EXPECT_THOUGHT, EXPECT_ACTION, EXPECT_OBSERVATION, EXPECT_ACTION_INPUT? Actually we can have a single state and expected type sequence.\n\nBetter: track the expected next type based on previous type. Also note that \"Action Input\" is a special type. We need to parse it as a separate record.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate case payload: has 'lines' key, list of strings.\n2. Initialize empty trajectory list, valid = True, violation_line = None, valid_prefix = None.\n3. Initialize expected_next = 'Thought' (or allow starting with Thought, but also maybe first line could be Final? No, because Final only at end. So first must be Thought.)\n4. Iterate over lines with index starting at 1.\n5. For each line, check it matches pattern: `^(Thought|Action|Action Input|Observation|Final): (.+)$` (case insensitive? The example has capital first letter. We'll preserve case but convert to lowercase in output? \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records\" - so output type should be lowercase. Also we might need to validate content if it's non-empty? We'll just capture.\n6. If not match, it's a violation. Set valid=False, violation_line = index, valid_prefix = list of records so far (the valid prefix). Break.\n7. Extract type and content. Normalize type to lowercase for comparison? But we need to compare with expected_next. The expected_next should be in lowercase.\n8. If type.lower() not in allowed types? Actually we only accept those five. If type is not one of those, violation.\n9. Check if type.lower() == 'final':\n   - If expected_next is not 'thought', then violation because Final only after Thought. Also if not at end? Actually we check after parsing all lines: if there are lines after Final, that's violation. But we can detect early: if type is Final, then we set a flag that we are in final state; any further lines are violation.\n   - Also if there are lines before first line is Final, violation (since first must be Thought).\n10. Otherwise, check if type.lower() matches expected_next:\n    - For 'thought': expected_next should be 'thought' (start) or after observation? Actually after observation, expected_next should be 'thought'.\n    - For 'action': expected_next should be 'action' (after thought, if not final).\n    - For 'action input': if expected_next is 'action', then we can allow it, but then expected_next becomes 'action_input'? Actually after Action Input, expected_next should be 'observation'. So we need to handle that. But the problem doesn't specify that Action Input is mandatory. So we need to allow both: after Action, next can be either Observation (if no action input) or Action Input. Similarly, after Action Input, next must be Observation.\n    - For 'observation': expected_next should be 'observation' (after action or action input).\n    - After observation, expected_next becomes 'thought'.\n    - Also, if we are in a state where final is allowed (after thought), then final is valid.\n11. If type.lower() matches, add record to trajectory. Update expected_next accordingly.\n    - After thought: expected_next = 'action' (or allow final? We'll set expected_next = 'action', but also allow final as alternative. We'll handle final separately by checking if type is final and expected_next is 'action'? Actually expected_next should store the primary expected. But we need to allow final as an alternative. So we'll have a variable `allowed_next` set? Simpler: after thought, expected_next = 'action', but we also check if type is 'final' and if we are at a valid point (after thought). So we'll have a condition: if type.lower() == 'final' and (expected_next == 'action' or expected_next == 'thought'? Actually after thought we have expected_next='action', but we want to allow final. So we need to check that final is allowed only when previous type was 'thought' (or at start? first line cannot be final). So we can track previous type. Better: keep previous type. Then validate based on sequence rules.\n12. If violation, set valid=False, record the line number and the prefix (trajectory up to previous line). Break.\n13. After loop, check if no violation. Also ensure that last line is not a non-terminal (e.g., last line should be either Observation (if followed by thought) or Final? Actually if last line is Observation, then next expected would be Thought, so it's incomplete. That should be a violation? The problem says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" It doesn't say that a cycle must be complete. But we should consider incomplete trajectory? The validation: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" If the trajectory ends without a Final, is that a violation? Possibly yes, because it's not a complete trajectory? But the example ends with Final. I think we should enforce that the entire sequence ends with Final. Check spec: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" That implies that Final must be the last line. But it doesn't say that a trajectory without Final is invalid. However, the typical ReAct trajectory ends with Final to output answer. The input may be incomplete? The task says \"Parse and validate\" - we should raise ValueError for structurally invalid? But the case payload is provided; we are not to repair. The instruction says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". So if the case is incomplete (e.g., no Final), that might be structurally invalid. I'll assume that a valid trajectory must end with a Final record. But let's check the test cases: they all have Final at the end. So we can enforce that the last line's type is 'final'. Also, the number of lines must be >=? Possibly at least 1. If empty lines list, that's invalid.\n\nAlso, we need to output the trajectory with type lowercase. Ex: \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\". Action Input would be \"action_input\".\n\nWe need to return a JSON object with keys: valid (bool), trajectory (list of dicts with type, content, line), and maybe error info? The problem says: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So in case of violation, we should include violation_line and valid_prefix (the list of records up to the line before violation). The valid_prefix is the trajectory so far. So for valid case, we might not include violation_line. But we need to return the trajectory and valid flag. Also preserve source identifiers: maybe case_id? But not specified. The function only gets case dict. We can include case_id from case['case_id'] if present, but not necessary. The output should be JSON-serializable.\n\nLet's design output:\nFor valid:\n{\n  \"valid\": true,\n  \"trajectory\": [{\"type\": \"thought\", \"content\": \"inspect\", \"line\": 1}, ...]\n}\nFor invalid:\n{\n  \"valid\": false,\n  \"violation_line\": 2,\n  \"valid_prefix\": [{\"type\": \"thought\", \"content\": \"inspect\", \"line\": 1}],\n  \"trajectory\": []? Or maybe we still include the partial trajectory? The problem says \"return the valid prefix\" which is the records up to that point. So we should include that as list. Also we might include the full trajectory? But it says \"on the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we should return those. The full trajectory including the violating line? Probably not. So we return valid_prefix as the list of records before violation. And we also need to return something else? Possibly we need to return the error line and the valid prefix. Also maybe the type of violation? Not required.\n\nHowever, the specification says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" So we must always return trajectory (list of records) and valid. For invalid, the trajectory should be the valid prefix? Or the full but with invalid line? \"valid prefix\" suggests the valid part. So I'll set trajectory to the valid prefix in invalid case. And include violation_line.\n\nBut the requirements: \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" That is the minimum. Additional fields may be present for audit. I'll include violation_line and valid_prefix as separate fields. But the valid_prefix is the same as trajectory in invalid case. To avoid duplication, just set trajectory to the valid prefix and set valid false. Also include violation_line. That should be fine.\n\nNow implement solve(case):\n- Input case is a dict. Ensure it has 'lines' and it's a list of strings. If not, raise ValueError.\n- Iterate as described.\n- Use regex: import re\n- pattern = r'^(Thought|Action|Action Input|Observation|Final): (.+)$'  # Note: Action Input has space\n- We'll use re.match with case-insensitive? Use re.IGNORECASE.\n- But we need to preserve original type string for output? Output type should be lowercase. So capture groups: type_str, content.\n- Then type_lower = type_str.lower().replace(' ', '_')? Actually \"Action Input\" becomes \"action_input\". So we can replace space with underscore.\n- Then proceed.\n\nState machine:\nInitialize:\ntrajectory = []\nvalid = True\nviolation_line = None\nprev_type = None\nidx = 0\nlines = case['lines']\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n    match = re.match(r'^(Thought|Action|Action Input|Observation|Final):\\s*(.+)$', line, re.IGNORECASE)\n    if not match:\n        valid = False; violation_line = i; break\n    type_str = match.group(1)\n    content = match.group(2).strip()  # maybe trim\n    type_lower = type_str.lower().replace(' ', '_')\n    # Validation rules:\n    # - First line must be Thought (or Final? No, first must be Thought)\n    if i == 1 and type_lower != 'thought':\n        valid = False; violation_line = i; break\n    # - After Final, no more lines (we'll detect later because we break on final? Actually we need to allow final and then check if there are lines after; we'll process normally then after loop check if last line is final and no lines after? But we are inside loop; if we encounter Final, we set a flag that we are in final state, then after processing that line, we check if there are more lines - but we are in loop, so next iteration will detect that line exists after final. We can set a flag `final_seen` and if we see another line after final, violation.\n    # We'll handle by checking if final_seen and then any subsequent line is violation.\n    # Also check ordering:\n    if not re.match(r'^(thought|action|action_input|observation|final)$', type_lower):\n        valid = False; violation_line = i; break\n    # Determine expected next based on prev_type\n    if prev_type is None:\n        # first line must be thought (already checked)\n        expected = 'thought'\n    else:\n        # define allowed transitions\n        if prev_type == 'thought':\n            allowed = ['action', 'final']\n        elif prev_type == 'action':\n            allowed = ['observation', 'action_input']\n        elif prev_type == 'action_input':\n            allowed = ['observation']\n        elif prev_type == 'observation':\n            allowed = ['thought']\n        elif prev_type == 'final':\n            allowed = []  # no more\n        else:\n            allowed = []\n        if type_lower not in allowed:\n            valid = False; violation_line = i; break\n    # Update\n    if type_lower == 'final':\n        # final is allowed only if prev_type == 'thought' (already covered by allowed)\n        pass\n    record = {'type': type_lower, 'content': content, 'line': i}\n    trajectory.append(record)\n    prev_type = type_lower\n    # If final, we can set a flag to ensure no more lines, but we'll catch in next iteration because allowed is empty.\n    # After loop, if no violation and we have at least one record, check that last type is 'final'\nIf valid and len(trajectory) > 0 and trajectory[-1]['type'] != 'final':\n    valid = False; violation_line = len(lines)+1? Actually the violation is that the trajectory does not end with final. The last line is not final, so the error line could be the last line? The problem says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" If the last line is not final, the violation is at the end. We could say violation_line = len(lines) (the last line), and valid_prefix is trajectory up to that line? But the valid prefix would be the whole trajectory? That might be fine. However, the spec says \"Permit Final only at the end\". So if the last line is not Final, it's a violation. We should set violation_line to the last line index and valid_prefix to the trajectory except the last? Or include the last? Since the last line is invalid, the valid prefix is the records before it. So we should not include the last line in valid_prefix. But we already appended it. So we need to adjust. So after loop, if valid but last type not final, we need to revert: valid = False, violation_line = len(lines), valid_prefix = trajectory[:-1]. But we also need to output trajectory as valid_prefix. So we can set trajectory = valid_prefix. Alternatively, we can check this condition before appending the last record? We can after loop, if we have trajectory and last type is not final, then we set valid false, violation_line = len(lines), and trajectory = trajectory[:-1] (the prefix). Then we break? That's fine.\n\nAlso need to handle case where lines list is empty: raise ValueError? Possibly invalid. We'll raise ValueError if lines not present or not list or empty? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". So if case is missing 'lines' or it's not a list, raise ValueError. If list is empty, that's structurally invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nNow implement.\n\nAlso note: \"Do not mutate the input.\" We are not.\n\nReturn: dict with keys: valid (bool), trajectory (list). For invalid, also include violation_line (int). That should satisfy.\n\nNow write solve function.\n\nWe need to output as JSON string inside \"implementation\". We'll write python source code as a string.\n\nWe must escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nThe output should be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python code.\n\nI'll produce the code.\n\nLet's write the solve function:\n\nimport re\n\ndef solve(case):\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'lines' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing 'lines'\")\n    lines = case['lines']\n    if not isinstance(lines, list) or not lines:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: lines must be non-empty list\")\n    trajectory = []\n    valid = True\n    violation_line = None\n    prev_type = None\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        match = re.match(r'^(Thought|Action|Action Input|Observation|Final):\\s*(.*)$', line, re.IGNORECASE)\n        if not match:\n            valid = False\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        type_str = match.group(1)\n        content = match.group(2).strip()\n        type_lower = type_str.lower().replace(' ', '_')\n        # Validate type against known\n        if type_lower not in ('thought', 'action', 'action_input', 'observation', 'final'):\n            valid = False\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        # First line must be thought\n        if i == 1 and type_lower != 'thought':\n            valid = False\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        # Sequence validation\n        if prev_type is None:\n            allowed = ['thought']\n        else:\n            if prev_type == 'thought':\n                allowed = ['action', 'final']\n            elif prev_type == 'action':\n                allowed = ['observation', 'action_input']\n            elif prev_type == 'action_input':\n                allowed = ['observation']\n            elif prev_type == 'observation':\n                allowed = ['thought']\n            elif prev_type == 'final':\n                allowed = []\n            else:\n                allowed = []\n        if type_lower not in allowed:\n            valid = False\n            violation_line = i\n            break\n        record = {'type': type_lower, 'content': content, 'line': i}\n        trajectory.append(record)\n        prev_type = type_lower\n    # After loop, check if valid and last line is not final\n    if valid and trajectory:\n        last_type = trajectory[-1]['type']\n        if last_type != 'final':\n            valid = False\n            violation_line = len(lines)  # last line index\n            # valid prefix is everything except the last record\n            trajectory = trajectory[:-1]  # remove the last invalid record\n    # If valid, ensure at least one record (should be by now)\n    if valid:\n        return {'valid': True, 'trajectory': trajectory}\n    else:\n        # valid_prefix is the trajectory up to violation (already set)\n        return {'valid': False, 'violation_line': violation_line, 'valid_prefix': trajectory}\n\nBut note: The valid_prefix should be the records before the violating line. In the loop, when we break, we have not added the violating record to trajectory if we break before appending? In my code, I check condition after parsing? Actually I break before appending if invalid. So trajectory at that point contains records up to previous line. That's correct. In the post-loop check, if valid but last not final, I remove the last record to get prefix. So trajectory ends up being valid prefix. That's fine.\n\nAlso, need to include the trajectory in both valid and invalid. For invalid, it's the valid prefix. So we can use trajectory as the key. The spec says \"return its one-based line and the valid prefix\". So we need to return violation_line and valid_prefix. We'll use keys 'violation_line' and 'valid_prefix' to match. Also 'valid' flag. Also 'trajectory'? The minimal requirement is \"lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" So we must return trajectory. In invalid case, the trajectory should be the valid prefix. We'll set 'trajectory' to that prefix. That satisfies both.\n\nSo final return dict will have 'valid', 'trajectory', and optionally 'violation_line' and 'valid_prefix' (which is same as trajectory). But to avoid duplication, we can just return 'valid' and 'trajectory' and 'violation_line'. Or include both. I'll include 'valid_prefix' as separate key for clarity, but it's redundant. The instruction: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we should explicitly include those keys. So for invalid, return: {'valid': False, 'violation_line': violation_line, 'valid_prefix': trajectory, 'trajectory': trajectory}? Actually 'trajectory' is the same as 'valid_prefix', but we can include both. To be safe, I'll include 'trajectory' (the valid prefix) and also 'violation_line' and 'valid_prefix' as separate. However, the instruction says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" So the trajectory is the main output. I'll return 'valid' and 'trajectory' in all cases. For invalid, I'll also include 'violation_line'. The 'valid_prefix' is implicit as the trajectory. So I'll skip 'valid_prefix' to keep it simple, but the problem says \"return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So maybe we need both. I'll include 'violation_line' and 'valid_prefix' (which equals trajectory). That covers.\n\nNow implement as a string.\n\nAlso need to handle potential edge cases: multiple spaces after colon? Our regex uses \\s*.\n\nAlso note: The lines may have extra spaces. We'll strip content.\n\nNow define solve inside the JSON object.\n\nLet's produce the final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":805,"completion_tokens":6373,"total_tokens":7178,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":512},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5794},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":293},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}